1. Configure a local security policy on your Windows Server to enforce password complexity requirements, then take a screenshot of the policy settings window.
=>Press Win + R, type secpol.msc, and press Enter.
Expand Account Policies → Password Policy.
Double-click Password must meet complexity requirements.
Select Enabled → click OK.
(Recommended companion settings you can also set in the same node):
Minimum password length = 8 (or higher)
Maximum password age = as needed
Take a screenshot of the Password Policy pane that clearly shows “Password must meet complexity requirements” set to Enabled.

2. Enable Windows Defender on your server and schedule a daily quick scan at a specific time using Windows Task Scheduler.<br><br><em><strong>Hint:</strong> Use the 'Task Scheduler' app and select 'Windows Defender Scheduled Scan' as the task.</em>
=>Open Windows Security (search for it) → Virus & threat protection and verify real-time protection is On. If it is off, turn it on.
Open Task Scheduler (taskschd.msc).
In the left pane go to:
Task Scheduler Library → Microsoft → Windows → Windows Defender.
Right-click Windows Defender Scheduled Scan → Properties.
On the Triggers tab → New:
Begin the task: On a schedule
Daily
Choose a specific time (e.g., 02:00)
Enabled → OK
On the Actions tab confirm the action is still “Start a program” with the Defender executable (or leave the existing action).
On the Settings / Conditions tabs adjust as needed (allow start on batteries, etc.).
Click OK. The task will now run a scan daily at the time you chose.

3. Set up a basic scheduled backup for a folder named 'ProjectFiles' on your server using Windows Server Backup, and save the backup to a different drive.
=>Install the feature if needed:
Server Manager → Add Roles and Features → Features → check Windows Server Backup → Install.
Open Windows Server Backup (search for it or wbadmin.msc).
In the right Actions pane click Backup Schedule….
Choose Custom → Next.
Click Add Items → select the folder ProjectFiles → OK → Next.
Choose a schedule.
Select Backup to a hard disk that is dedicated for backups or Backup to a volume on a different drive.
Confirm the destination drive and finish the wizard.
The scheduled backup will now appear under Scheduled Backup.

4. Install and configure Performance Monitor to track CPU and memory usage on your server for 30 minutes, then export the collected report as a screenshot or CSV file.
=>Open Performance Monitor (perfmon.msc).
Expand Data Collector Sets → User Defined.
Right-click User Defined → New → Data Collector Set.
Name it (e.g., “CPU-Memory-30min”) → Create manually (Advanced) → Next.
Select Performance Counter → Next → Add:
Processor → % Processor Time (instance _Total)
Memory → Available MBytes (or % Committed Bytes In Use)
Add any other counters you want → OK → Next.
Set sample interval (e.g., 15 seconds) → Next.
Choose a location to save the data → Next → Save and close or Start this data collector set now.
Right-click the new Data Collector Set → Start. Let it run for 30 minutes, then Stop.
To view / export:
Under Reports → User Defined open the report, or
Go to the log file location → the .blg file can be opened in Performance Monitor.

5. Write a short report (200-300 words) describing the security policies, backup schedule, and monitoring tools you configured, including any challenges faced and how you resolved them.
=>I configured a local password policy that enforces complexity requirements (enabled via secpol.msc under Account Policies → Password Policy). This forces passwords to contain a mix of character types and prevents simple or username-based passwords, significantly reducing the success rate of dictionary and brute-force attacks.
Windows Defender real-time protection was confirmed enabled. Using Task Scheduler I modified the existing “Windows Defender Scheduled Scan” task so that a quick scan runs daily at a fixed time (e.g., 02:00). This provides continuous malware protection with minimal administrative overhead.
A scheduled backup of the ProjectFiles folder was created with Windows Server Backup. The backup runs on a daily schedule and is stored on a separate drive, ensuring recovery capability if the primary volume fails or data is accidentally deleted.
Performance Monitor was used to create a custom Data Collector Set that logged % Processor Time and Available Memory for a continuous 30-minute period. The resulting report/graph (or exported CSV) allows baseline analysis of resource usage and can help identify performance bottlenecks.
Challenges & resolutions

The Windows Server Backup feature was not installed by default; it was added via Server Manager.
Ensuring the backup destination was on a different physical/logical drive required careful selection in the wizard.
The Performance Monitor data collector needed an explicit stop after 30 minutes; this was handled by starting and stopping the set manually (or by setting a duration stop condition).

These configurations together improve password strength, malware protection, data recoverability, and visibility into system performance.
